WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Econ 10-20, Principles of Microeconomics, an audio study guide.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.680
This covers your whole course chapter by chapter.

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.100
Because you're listening rather than looking, every graph gets

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.220
described out loud.

5
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.800
So picture it as I go.

6
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.380
If you can sketch along, even better, there are 11 sections.

7
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.260
Let's start.

8
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.140
Section 1, How to Actually Pass This Class.

9
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.820
Your grade is 80% exams, 20% problem sets, plus up to 3% extra

10
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.880
credit from Top Hat.

11
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.640
All three exams are multiple choice in class.

12
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.440
Closed note, your best exam counts for 30% and the other two

13
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.960
count 25% each.

14
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.240
Or, you can skip the third midterm and take a comprehensive

15
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.820
final instead, which is worth 40% if it helps your grade.

16
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.360
Midterm 1 is September 30th, a Wednesday, in class.

17
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.300
Midterm 2 is November 4th, also a Wednesday.

18
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.660
Midterm 3, or the final, happens in the official exam slot in

19
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:54.000
December.

20
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.820
Two things in the syllabus quietly protect you.

21
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.240
Your lowest problem set score is dropped, and if you miss any

22
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.220
entirely, you can email them at the end of the term for 50%

23
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:03.680
credit.

24
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.040
And about five classes worth of Top Hat points are excused.

25
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.580
But there are no problem set extensions, for any reason, ever.

26
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.140
Now, the study method Professor Strom actually recommends.

27
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.560
There are four steps.

28
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:16.320
Step 1.

29
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.820
Review each slide deck right after lecture.

30
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.360
Be ruthless about this.

31
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.980
Ask yourself, could I explain every single point on this slide

32
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.340
to a friend, out loud, with no notes?

33
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.800
If the answer is no, you don't know it yet.

34
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.280
He says 95% of students fool themselves at exactly this step.

35
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:33.940
Step 2.

36
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.240
Do the problem sets alone first, before asking anyone.

37
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.180
Every problem he assigns is deliberately targeting one specific

38
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.040
building block.

39
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.820
So as you work, ask yourself, which concept is this problem

40
00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:46.240
testing?

41
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.400
Then go to a friend if you're stuck, then a teaching assistant.

42
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:50.140
Step 3.

43
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.520
Read the answer key carefully, including for the questions you

44
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:53.960
got right.

45
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.300
Getting the right answer for the wrong reason still costs you on

46
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:57.780
the exam.

47
00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:58.640
Step 4.

48
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.660
Only after all of that, do you practice exams?

49
00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:02.420
Timed.

50
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.400
No notes, like the real thing.

51
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.160
Then modify every question.

52
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.540
Ask, what would have to change for a different answer to be

53
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:09.920
correct?

54
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.380
Can you reverse the question?

55
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.440
Jeopardy style?

56
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.560
Can you swap in a related concept?

57
00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.740
Here's the single most important idea about this course.

58
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.780
Memorizers do fine right up, until a question asks them to do

59
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.140
something new with a definition.

60
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.480
Then they stumble, and they complain the exam-covered things

61
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.420
that weren't in class.

62
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.480
The top grades go to students who learn the connections between

63
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.240
the building blocks, not just the blocks themselves.

64
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.620
This audio guide gives you the blocks.

65
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.080
You build the connections by doing problems.

66
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.000
One note, he specifically asks that you do that step, four

67
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.340
-question modifying yourself, rather than having artificial

68
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.000
intelligence generate practice questions for you.

69
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.380
The critical thinking in that process is the whole point, so use

70
00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.040
this to learn the material fast.

71
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.920
But do those reps on your own.

72
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:55.780
Section 2.

73
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.260
Chapters 1 and 2.

74
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.480
What Economics is?

75
00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.300
This is the vocabulary chapter.

76
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.820
It's chi points on midterm 1, so just memorize it.

77
00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.180
Economics is the study of how agents choose to allocate scarce

78
00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.120
resources, and how those choices affect society.

79
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.700
An economic agent is anyone making choices.

80
00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:13.660
A person.

81
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:14.440
A household.

82
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:15.280
A firm.

83
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.140
A government.

84
00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.420
Microeconomics zooms in on individuals, households, firms, and

85
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.120
single markets.

86
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.980
A micro question sounds like, why did coffee prices rise?

87
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.280
Macroeconomics looks at the whole economy.

88
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.180
A macro question sounds like, why is unemployment at 4%?

89
00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.160
Next, positive versus normative economics.

90
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:38.380
Positive economics is objective description, prediction, and

91
00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:38.740
fact.

92
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.340
The key word is falsifiable.

93
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.540
It could, in principle, be checked against evidence.

94
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.400
For example, a $15 minimum wage reduces teen employment by 2%.

95
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.320
Normative economics is about what should or ought to happen,

96
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.200
from a moral or value standpoint.

97
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:54.320
It's subjective.

98
00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.460
For example, the minimum wage should be $15.

99
00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:58.920
Here's the trap.

100
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.300
A positive statement can be completely wrong and still be

101
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:02.680
positive.

102
00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.440
What makes it positive is that it could be tested.

103
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:06.640
Not that it's true.

104
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.340
Students lose points by assuming false equals normative.

105
00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.220
Now the three principles that frame the entire course.

106
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.720
Principle 1 is optimization.

107
00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.760
Agents choose whatever gives them the best value.

108
00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.220
That's chapter 3.

109
00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:20.780
Principle 2 is equilibrium.

110
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.800
And this definition has two halves.

111
00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.220
Everyone is optimizing.

112
00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.540
And nobody believes they would personally be better off changing

113
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.360
their own choice.

114
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.600
Holding everyone else's choices fixed.

115
00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:31.960
Memorize both halves.

116
00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.320
The second half is the part students drop.

117
00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.240
And it's the part that does the work later in game theory.

118
00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:38.960
Principle 3 is empiricism.

119
00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.460
Which just means evidence-based analysis.

120
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.680
Economics mostly can't run controlled lab experiments.

121
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.580
For ethical and practical reasons.

122
00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.200
You can't randomly assign people to be poor.

123
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.560
So economics answers cause and effect questions two ways.

124
00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.580
The theory-driven approach builds a model with no data.

125
00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.340
Changes one thing and sees what happens.

126
00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.020
The statistics-driven approach finds clever ways to imitate a

127
00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.140
controlled experiment using real-world observational data.

128
00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.140
Actual research sits on a spectrum between those two.

129
00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:09.060
Section 3.

130
00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.040
Chapter 3.

131
00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.480
Optimization and Opportunity Cost.

132
00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.880
Opportunity cost is the value of the best-forgone alternative.

133
00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.780
In this course you use it in exactly two ways.

134
00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.440
First, as a broad idea.

135
00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.520
Always ask what you're giving up.

136
00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:25.600
Second, as a way to put a dollar value on time.

137
00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:29.200
An hour spent commuting costs you whatever that hour was worth.

138
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.680
Usually your wage.

139
00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.940
Some cost is money or time already spent that you cannot get

140
00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:34.300
back.

141
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.060
Ignore it completely when deciding what to do next.

142
00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.880
Only future costs and future benefits matter.

143
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.420
If you've already paid $40 for a concert ticket and you feel

144
00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.740
sick, the $40 is gone either way.

145
00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.740
The only question is whether going tonight beats staying home

146
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:49.100
tonight.

147
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.220
Now, cost-benefit analysis.

148
00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.880
Net benefit equals total benefit minus total cost.

149
00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.640
The optimum is wherever net benefit is highest.

150
00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.340
There are two routes to find it.

151
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.240
Total analysis.

152
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.580
Compute the net benefit of every option and pick the biggest

153
00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:03.960
number.

154
00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:05.180
Marginal analysis.

155
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.500
Look at one extra unit at a time.

156
00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.800
Keep going as long as marginal benefit is at least as large as

157
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:11.580
marginal cost.

158
00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:14.740
Stop when marginal benefit equals marginal cost.

159
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.400
This is the method the entire rest of the course uses.

160
00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.080
Let me walk through an example.

161
00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.360
Suppose you're deciding how many hours to study, and each hour

162
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.480
costs you $20 of forgone work.

163
00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:28.320
The first hour gives you $60 of benefit and costs $20.

164
00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.280
Net benefit so far, $40.

165
00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.440
The second hour gives you $40 of additional benefit and costs

166
00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:34.940
$20.

167
00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.140
Cumulative net benefit, $60.

168
00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:41.640
The third hour gives you $25 of benefit and costs $20.

169
00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.080
Cumulative net benefit, $65.

170
00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.400
The fourth hour gives you only $10 of benefit and still costs

171
00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:48.840
$20.

172
00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.500
Cumulative net benefit drops to $55.

173
00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.280
Total analysis says, net benefit peaks at 3 hours, at $65.

174
00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.500
Marginal analysis says, our 3 is worth taking, because 25 is

175
00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.360
greater than 20.

176
00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.480
Our 4 is not, because 10 is less than 20.

177
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:06.640
So stop at 3.

178
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:07.660
Same answer.

179
00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:08.840
Far less arithmetic.

180
00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.040
That's why marginal analysis wins.

181
00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.100
The trap here.

182
00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.500
Marginal benefit falls as you do more of something, and marginal

183
00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.800
cost usually rises.

184
00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.860
Your optimum is where they cross.

185
00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:22.380
It is not where total benefit is highest, and it is not where

186
00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:23.320
cost is lowest.

187
00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:24.360
Section 4.

188
00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:25.300
Chapter 4.

189
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.040
Supply.

190
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:26.880
Demand.

191
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:27.880
And Equilibrium.

192
00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.080
This is the workhorse model of the course.

193
00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.420
Expect the most exam questions here.

194
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.740
A market is buyers and sellers of a good.

195
00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.940
Ceteris paribus means holding everything else constant.

196
00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.200
That assumption is what lets us draw a curve in two dimensions

197
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.320
when reality has many moving parts.

198
00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:44.840
Let's set up the picture.

199
00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.540
Price is on the vertical axis.

200
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.680
Quantity is on the horizontal axis.

201
00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.640
The demand curve slopes downward.

202
00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:51.680
Higher price.

203
00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:53.020
Lower quantity demanded.

204
00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:54.600
And here's the crucial part.

205
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.100
Read the height of the demand curve as willingness to pay, which

206
00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:59.620
is the buyer's marginal benefit.

207
00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.540
The supply curve slopes upward.

208
00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:02.520
Higher price.

209
00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:03.900
Higher quantity supplied.

210
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.820
Read the height of the supply curve as willingness to accept,

211
00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:08.980
which is the seller's marginal cost.

212
00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:12.560
To aggregate either curve from individuals to a market, you add

213
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.680
quantities horizontally at each price.

214
00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:15.640
Horizontally.

215
00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:16.480
Not vertically.

216
00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.200
Where the two curves cross.

217
00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:19.060
That's equilibrium.

218
00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.020
Call the price there P star and the quantity Q star.

219
00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.820
Now picture a horizontal line above P star.

220
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.360
At that higher price, sellers want to sell a lot and buyers want

221
00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:28.960
to buy little.

222
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.640
Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded.

223
00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:32.880
That's excess supply.

224
00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:33.720
A surplus.

225
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.120
And sellers cut their price to move inventory.

226
00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:39.000
Now picture a horizontal line below P star.

227
00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:40.400
Buyers want a lot.

228
00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:41.520
Sellers offer little.

229
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:42.600
That's excess demand.

230
00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:43.400
A shortage.

231
00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.020
And buyers bid the price up.

232
00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.160
Only at P star does nobody want to change what they're doing.

233
00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:51.760
Which is exactly the definition of equilibrium from chapter 1.

234
00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.560
Now the single most tested idea in this chapter.

235
00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.380
Movement along a curve versus a shift of the curve.

236
00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.840
If the goods own price changed, you move along the existing

237
00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:01.260
curve.

238
00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.280
If anything else changed, the whole curve shifts.

239
00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.300
If price changed on its own, do not shift the curve.

240
00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:08.620
What shifts demand?

241
00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:09.340
Income.

242
00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.880
And here you need to know normal goods versus inferior goods.

243
00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:16.220
The price of substitutes, which are goods used instead of each

244
00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:17.480
other, like Coke and Pepsi.

245
00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.940
The price of complements, which are goods used together, like

246
00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:21.820
printers and ink.

247
00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.220
Plus tastes, expectations, and the number of buyers.

248
00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.680
What shifts supply?

249
00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:30.700
Input prices, technology, expectations, and the number of

250
00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.020
sellers.

251
00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:34.480
Here's what happens to price and quantity when curves shift.

252
00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:37.880
If demand rises, both price and quantity rise.

253
00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.020
If demand falls, both fall.

254
00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.220
If supply rises, price falls and quantity rises.

255
00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.720
If supply falls, price rises and quantity falls.

256
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.120
Now the harder case, when both curves shift at once.

257
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.220
If demand rises and supply rises, quantity definitely rises.

258
00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.380
But the effect on price is ambiguous.

259
00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:00.140
If demand rises and supply falls, price definitely rises.

260
00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:01.820
But quantity is ambiguous.

261
00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:05.700
If demand falls and supply rises, price definitely falls.

262
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:07.000
Quantity ambiguous.

263
00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:10.980
If demand falls and supply falls, quantity definitely falls.

264
00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:11.920
Price ambiguous.

265
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.240
Here's the shortcut so you don't have to memorize four cases.

266
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.200
When both curves shift, look at which effect, price or quantity,

267
00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.380
the two shifts push in the same direction.

268
00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:22.780
That one is certain.

269
00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:26.640
The other one is ambiguous because it depends on which shift was

270
00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:26.940
bigger.

271
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:29.340
Finally, price ceilings and price floors.

272
00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:33.040
An effective price ceiling sits below the equilibrium price.

273
00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.240
Rent control is the classic example.

274
00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.280
Picture a horizontal line below where the curves cross.

275
00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.360
At that low price, buyers want a large quantity but sellers only

276
00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:43.340
offer a small one.

277
00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.820
The result is excess demand, a shortage.

278
00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:49.520
And trade actually happens only up to the quantity supplied

279
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.560
because sellers simply will not produce more than that.

280
00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.960
The model doesn't tell you anything beyond that.

281
00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.680
There's a shortage with upward pressure on price.

282
00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:00.600
An effective price floor sits above equilibrium.

283
00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:02.920
The minimum wage is the classic example.

284
00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:04.180
It's the mirror image.

285
00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:05.780
The result is excess supply.

286
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.040
And trade happens only up to the quantity demanded, with

287
00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.480
downward pressure on price.

288
00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:11.700
Two warnings here.

289
00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:15.800
First, if a ceiling is set above equilibrium, or a floor below

290
00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.520
it, nothing happens at all.

291
00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:18.520
It isn't binding.

292
00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:22.260
Second, students constantly flip the words shortage and surplus

293
00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:23.340
under exam pressure.

294
00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.920
So reason it out from excess demand and excess supply instead of

295
00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.100
trying to recall the label.

296
00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.540
Draw the horizontal line.

297
00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:30.400
Look at the gap.

298
00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:31.300
Then name it.

299
00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:32.280
Section 5.

300
00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:33.420
Chapter 5.

301
00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.060
Consumers, Surplus, and Elasticity.

302
00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:39.320
Elasticity is the most calculation-heavy topic on the early

303
00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:39.820
exams.

304
00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.180
So drill the formula until it's automatic.

305
00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.200
First, the budget constraint.

306
00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:47.280
It's the line showing every combination of two goods you can

307
00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:48.220
exactly afford.

308
00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:52.960
With goods X and Y, it's the price of X times the quantity of X,

309
00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:57.180
plus the price of Y times the quantity of Y, equals your income.

310
00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:00.740
If your income rises, the line shifts outward, parallel to

311
00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:01.100
itself.

312
00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:02.620
The slope doesn't change.

313
00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:06.340
If the price of just one good falls, the line pivots outward on

314
00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:09.180
that goods axis only, and the slope does change.

315
00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:12.720
The slope itself is the price ratio, which is the rate at which

316
00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:14.940
the market lets you trade one good for the other.

317
00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.980
Next, bang for your buck.

318
00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:20.720
Optimization, at the margin says, keep spending on whichever

319
00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:23.080
good gives you more marginal benefit per dollar.

320
00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.740
You are optimizing when the marginal benefit of X divided by the

321
00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.580
price of X equals the marginal benefit of Y divided by the price

322
00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:31.040
of Y.

323
00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.840
If one side is bigger, buy more of that good.

324
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.620
Now, consumer surplus.

325
00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.860
Consumer surplus is what you were willing to pay minus what you

326
00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:41.620
actually paid, summed over every unit you bought.

327
00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:45.060
From a table, subtract the price from each unit's willingness to

328
00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:47.200
pay, and add up the positive amounts.

329
00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:48.860
Suppose the price is $6.

330
00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:53.280
The first unit you value at $10, so that's $4 of surplus.

331
00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.860
The second you value at $8, that's $2.

332
00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.380
The third you value at exactly $6, that's zero surplus, and

333
00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:00.860
you're indifferent.

334
00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.760
The fourth you value at $4, which is below the price, so you

335
00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:05.460
don't buy it.

336
00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.800
Your total consumer surplus is $6.

337
00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.480
From a graph, consumer surplus is the area below the demand

338
00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:14.700
curve and above the price line, out to the quantity traded.

339
00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:18.300
It's usually a triangle, so it's one-half times base times

340
00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:18.680
height.

341
00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:19.800
Now, elasticity.

342
00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:22.200
Elasticity answers one question.

343
00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:26.180
By what percent does X respond when Y changes by 1%?

344
00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:29.900
It's the percent change in X divided by the percent change in Y.

345
00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:33.340
Because percent changes depend on which point you start from,

346
00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:37.560
this course always uses the midpoint formula, sometimes called

347
00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:38.260
arc elasticity.

348
00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:39.340
Here it is.

349
00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.900
The numerator is quantity 2 minus quantity 1, divided by the

350
00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:46.140
average of quantity 1 and quantity 2.

351
00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.500
The denominator is price 2 minus price 1, divided by the average

352
00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:52.120
of price 1 and price 2.

353
00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.280
That is the only elasticity formula you need in this class,

354
00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:58.220
unless the percent changes are handed to you directly.

355
00:13:58.220 --> 00:13:59.400
Let's do one.

356
00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:04.340
Price rises from $4 to $6, and quantity falls from $100 to $80.

357
00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:08.200
The change in quantity is negative 20, and the average quantity

358
00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:12.840
is 90, so the percent change in quantity is negative 22.2%.

359
00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.900
The change in price is 2, and the average price is 5, so the

360
00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:19.060
percent change in price is 40%.

361
00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:19.600
Divide.

362
00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:24.300
Negative 22.2 divided by 40 gives negative 0.56.

363
00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:28.600
The absolute value is less than 1, so demand here is inelastic.

364
00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.220
Here is how to read the number.

365
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:34.600
If the absolute value of elasticity is greater than 1, demand is

366
00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:37.380
elastic, meaning quantity reacts a lot.

367
00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.120
If it equals 1, demand is unit elastic.

368
00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:44.280
If it's less than 1, demand is inelastic, meaning quantity

369
00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:48.280
barely reacts, and this connects directly to revenue, which they

370
00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.060
love to test.

371
00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:53.020
If demand is inelastic and you raise price, total revenue rises.

372
00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:57.140
If demand is elastic and you raise price, total revenue falls.

373
00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:00.360
At unit elasticity, revenue is at its maximum.

374
00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:01.420
The rule of thumb.

375
00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:05.100
Revenue moves in the same direction as price when demand is

376
00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:08.900
inelastic, and in the opposite direction when demand is elastic.

377
00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.120
Now a subtle point that trips people up.

378
00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:12.760
Elasticity is not slope.

379
00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:15.660
On a straight-line demand curve, the slope is constant

380
00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:18.880
everywhere, but the elasticity changes as you move along it.

381
00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.080
Up at the top, where price is high and few buyers remain.

382
00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.200
Demand is elastic, exactly at the midpoint.

383
00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:26.560
It's unit elastic.

384
00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:29.940
Down at the bottom, where price is low and only the most devoted

385
00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:30.840
buyers are left.

386
00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.360
It's inelastic, and total revenue is largest right at that

387
00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:34.840
midpoint.

388
00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.160
The extreme cases.

389
00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:39.140
A perfectly inelastic demand curve is vertical.

390
00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:40.520
Elasticity zero.

391
00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.080
Quantity never changes no matter the price.

392
00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.860
A perfectly elastic demand curve is horizontal.

393
00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:47.260
Elasticity infinite.

394
00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:50.020
Any price increase kills all your sales.

395
00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:52.120
What makes demand more elastic?

396
00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:54.100
More close substitutes available.

397
00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:56.700
The good being a luxury rather than a necessity.

398
00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.160
The good eating up a large share of your budget.

399
00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:00.980
Having a longer time to adjust.

400
00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:02.900
And narrower market definitions.

401
00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.560
Honda Civics have more elastic demand than cars in general,

402
00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:09.400
because you have more substitutes for a Civic than for

403
00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:10.560
transportation itself.

404
00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:11.980
Finally, two other elasticities.

405
00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:16.320
Cross-price elasticity of demand is the percent change in

406
00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.560
quantity demanded of good divided by the percent change in the

407
00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:20.400
price of good B.

408
00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:23.020
If it's positive, the goods are substitutes.

409
00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:25.040
If it's negative, they're complements.

410
00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:28.820
Income elasticity of demand is the percent change in quantity

411
00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:31.460
demanded divided by the percent change in income.

412
00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:33.400
If positive, it's a normal good.

413
00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:35.760
If negative, it's an inferior good.

414
00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:36.760
Section 6.

415
00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:37.760
Chapter 6.

416
00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.620
Producers and Cost Curbs.

417
00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.460
Lots of formulas here, but they're all just definitions.

418
00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:44.560
Learn the graph and the rest follows.

419
00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:45.920
Vocabulary first.

420
00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:48.620
In the short run, at least one input is fixed.

421
00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.480
In the long run, everything is variable, including whether the

422
00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:53.640
firm exists at all.

423
00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:57.240
The law of diminishing returns says that adding more of one

424
00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.720
input to fixed inputs eventually increases output by smaller and

425
00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:01.500
smaller amounts.

426
00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:03.000
10 cooks, 1 oven.

427
00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.200
This law is the reason marginal cost eventually rises.

428
00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:10.540
Economic profit subtracts all opportunity costs, including the

429
00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:12.340
salary you gave up to start the business.

430
00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:16.320
Accounting profit only subtracts explicit money costs, so

431
00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:19.140
economic profit is always less than or equal to accounting

432
00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:19.600
profit.

433
00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:23.480
Economies of scale means long run average cost falls as the firm

434
00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:24.100
gets bigger.

435
00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:26.380
Dis-economies of scale means it rises.

436
00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:27.700
Now the formulas.

437
00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.300
Marginal product equals the change in output divided by the

438
00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:32.100
change in labor.

439
00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:36.000
Total cost equals total fixed cost plus total variable cost.

440
00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:39.660
Marginal cost equals the change in total cost divided by the

441
00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:40.480
change in quantity.

442
00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:44.440
Average total cost equals total cost divided by quantity.

443
00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:48.720
Average variable cost equals total variable cost divided by

444
00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:49.180
quantity.

445
00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:53.500
Average fixed cost equals total fixed cost divided by quantity.

446
00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:56.280
Total revenue equals price times quantity.

447
00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:00.580
And profit equals total revenue minus total cost, which can also

448
00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:03.480
be written as quantity times the difference between price and

449
00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:04.560
average total cost.

450
00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.760
Here's a trap he warns about directly.

451
00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:10.480
When you compute marginals from a table, divide by the change in

452
00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:10.900
quantity.

453
00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.480
Don't just subtract one row from the next.

454
00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:18.200
If output jumps from 10 units to 14 units, marginal cost is the

455
00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:21.640
change in total cost divided by 4, not the raw difference.

456
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.880
Now picture the cost curve graph.

457
00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:27.240
Dollars on the vertical axis, quantity on the horizontal.

458
00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:30.360
Marginal cost is a curve that dips slightly and then rises

459
00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:30.880
steeply.

460
00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:34.780
Average variable cost is U-shaped, sitting below average total

461
00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:35.240
cost.

462
00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:39.480
Average total cost is also U-shaped, starting high and coming

463
00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:40.820
down before turning up again.

464
00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:42.240
The key visual fact.

465
00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:46.300
Marginal cost always cuts through both average variable cost and

466
00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:48.820
average total cost at their exact minimum points.

467
00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:50.240
That isn't a coincidence.

468
00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:54.240
An average falls whenever the next value is below it and rises

469
00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.060
whenever the next value is above it.

470
00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:59.640
So the average must be flat, at its minimum, exactly where

471
00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:00.860
marginal cost crosses.

472
00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.060
And the vertical gap between average total cost and average

473
00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:07.760
variable cost is average fixed cost, which shrinks forever as

474
00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:10.480
fixed costs get spread over more and more units.

475
00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:12.300
Now, how much to produce?

476
00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.440
Produce where marginal revenue equals marginal cost.

477
00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:16.980
Under perfect competition.

478
00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:19.220
Price equals marginal revenue.

479
00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:20.740
So that simplifies to.

480
00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.580
Produce where price equals marginal cost.

481
00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:25.240
Under perfect competition.

482
00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.480
Price also equals average revenue.

483
00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:28.940
Are you making a profit?

484
00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:31.820
Compare price to average total cost at that quantity.

485
00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:35.020
If price is above average total cost, you profit.

486
00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:36.740
If below, you lose.

487
00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:38.800
The size is quantity times the gap.

488
00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:40.040
Should you operate at all.

489
00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:41.040
In the short run.

490
00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.620
Shut down if price is below minimum average variable cost.

491
00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:45.420
Above that.

492
00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:48.540
You're at least covering your variable costs and chipping away

493
00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:51.080
at fixed costs, which are sunk in the short run.

494
00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:52.100
In the long run.

495
00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:55.140
Exit if price is below minimum average total cost.

496
00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:56.820
Because nothing is fixed anymore.

497
00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.380
And the break-even price is exactly minimum average total cost.

498
00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:03.140
Where economic profit is precisely zero.

499
00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:05.920
The firm's supply curve is its marginal cost curve.

500
00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.980
Above the relevant shutdown point.

501
00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.960
Add up all the firms horizontally to get market supply.

502
00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:12.180
In the long run.

503
00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.420
Positive profits attract entry.

504
00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:16.640
Entry shifts market supply to the right.

505
00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:18.280
And that pushes price down.

506
00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:22.080
This continues until price equals minimum long run average total

507
00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.120
cost and economic profit is zero.

508
00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:27.780
That zero profit price is the long run equilibrium price.

509
00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:31.280
Producer surplus is the area above the marginal cost curve.

510
00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:32.380
Below the price.

511
00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:33.820
Up to the quantity sold.

512
00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.180
It's the mirror image of consumer surplus.

513
00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:36.780
Finally.

514
00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.720
Elasticity of supply is the percent change in quantity supply

515
00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:42.920
divided by the percent change in price.

516
00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:44.080
To reason about it.

517
00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:45.400
Ask one question.

518
00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.720
Does this make the producer more flexible?

519
00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:49.740
More flexible means more elastic.

520
00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:53.580
Holding inventories make supply more elastic and perishable

521
00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:54.580
goods less so.

522
00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:57.220
The long run is more elastic than the short run.

523
00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:00.460
Heavy reliance on fixed inputs makes supply less elastic.

524
00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.540
And if variable inputs are easy to hire and adjust.

525
00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:05.080
Supply is more elastic.

526
00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.080
Section 7.

527
00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:07.120
Chapter 7.

528
00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:08.900
The Invisible Hand and Efficiency.

529
00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:10.040
For this chapter.

530
00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:11.960
Favor the slides over the textbook.

531
00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:13.360
He says so explicitly.

532
00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:18.240
And says the book's model variations in sections 7.1 through 7.3

533
00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:20.800
are more confusing than they're worth for an intro student.

534
00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:24.120
Decentralized decision making means each buyer and seller

535
00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:25.200
chooses for themselves.

536
00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:26.720
Guided by prices.

537
00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:29.680
Centralized means a planner decides for everyone.

538
00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:33.040
The invisible hand is the result that decentralized, self

539
00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:36.040
-interested choices can produce a socially good outcome.

540
00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:37.440
Nobody intends it.

541
00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.300
And prices do all the coordinating.

542
00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.160
Now picture the supply and demand graph again.

543
00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:43.200
At equilibrium.

544
00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:47.160
Consumer surplus is the triangle above the price line and below

545
00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:48.060
the demand curve.

546
00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:51.520
Producer surplus is the triangle below the price line and above

547
00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:52.500
the supply curve.

548
00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:54.860
Add them together and you get total surplus.

549
00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:57.760
Which is the entire area between the two curves.

550
00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:00.020
To the left of the equilibrium quantity.

551
00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:02.880
Why is the equilibrium quantity the right one?

552
00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:06.040
Because every unit to the left of it has marginal benefit above

553
00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:06.880
marginal cost.

554
00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.160
So it's worth trading.

555
00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.200
And every unit to the right costs more to produce than it's

556
00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:11.940
worth to anyone.

557
00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:15.720
A perfectly competitive market lands exactly on that quantity

558
00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.980
and therefore maximizes total surplus.

559
00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:21.200
That is precisely what efficient means in this course.

560
00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:24.620
Deadweight loss is the surplus destroyed when a market does not

561
00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.000
trade the efficient quantity.

562
00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:29.720
Think of it as the gap between what the market would do and what

563
00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:30.300
it should do.

564
00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:33.240
On a graph it's the triangle between the demand and supply

565
00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:33.740
curves.

566
00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.060
Spanning the units that fail to trade.

567
00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.620
Where that shouldn't have traded.

568
00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:40.200
The area is one half times base times height.

569
00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:43.560
Where the base is the vertical gap between marginal benefit and

570
00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:44.360
marginal cost.

571
00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:46.520
And the height is the quantity distortion.

572
00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:47.880
Free related terms.

573
00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:49.560
Pareto efficient means.

574
00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.900
You cannot make anyone better off without making someone else

575
00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:53.480
worse off.

576
00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:56.900
A Pareto improvement is a change that makes at least one person

577
00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:57.540
better off.

578
00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:58.920
And nobody worse off.

579
00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:02.380
And the equity efficiency trade off captures the idea that the

580
00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:05.320
biggest pie is not necessarily the fairly sliced pie.

581
00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:08.140
Efficiency is about the size of total surplus.

582
00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:09.960
Equity is about who gets it.

583
00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:12.140
Policies often trade one for the other.

584
00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.880
And economics alone cannot tell you the right balance.

585
00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:16.860
Because that's a normative question.

586
00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:17.700
Section 8.

587
00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:18.520
Chapter 9.

588
00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:19.560
Externalities.

589
00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:22.000
Note that Chapter 8 is skipped in this course.

590
00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:24.560
Because the topic belongs to intro macro.

591
00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:26.640
An externality is a spillover.

592
00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:30.220
It's a cost or a benefit that lands on someone who was not part

593
00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:30.960
of the transaction.

594
00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:32.420
The market ignores it.

595
00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:34.820
Which means the market gets the quantity wrong.

596
00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:36.900
There are four curves to keep straight.

597
00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:39.720
Marginal private benefit is the value to the buyer.

598
00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:41.660
And that is simply the demand curve.

599
00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:45.340
Marginal social benefit is marginal private benefit plus any

600
00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.200
benefits spilling onto everyone else.

601
00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:49.800
Marginal private cost is the cost to the seller.

602
00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:51.780
And that is simply the supply curve.

603
00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:55.600
Marginal social cost is marginal private cost plus any costs

604
00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:57.000
spilling onto everyone else.

605
00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:00.260
Private parties trade where marginal private benefit equals

606
00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:01.520
marginal private cost.

607
00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:05.060
Society's optimum is where marginal social benefit equals

608
00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:06.420
marginal social cost.

609
00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:09.420
When those two points differ, you get deadweight loss.

610
00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:11.320
Picture a negative externality.

611
00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:12.980
Like a factory polluting.

612
00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:16.000
The true cost to society exceeds the private cost.

613
00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:19.860
So the marginal social cost curve sits above the supply curve.

614
00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.780
The market crosses at a quantity to the right of the socially

615
00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:23.640
optimal quantity.

616
00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.300
So the market overproduces.

617
00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:28.680
The deadweight loss is a triangle between the market quantity

618
00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:30.040
and the optimal quantity.

619
00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:33.280
Made up of units whose social cost exceeded their social

620
00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:33.640
benefit.

621
00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:36.020
Now flip it for a positive externality.

622
00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:37.900
Like vaccines or education.

623
00:24:38.260 --> 00:24:41.160
The marginal social benefit sits above the demand curve.

624
00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.160
The optimal quantity is to the right of the market quantity.

625
00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:45.780
And the market underproduces.

626
00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:47.060
How do you fix it?

627
00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:48.680
Two families of solutions.

628
00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:50.340
Private solutions first.

629
00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:53.140
The Coase theorem says that if property rights are clearly

630
00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:55.520
assigned and transaction costs are low,

631
00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.620
the parties can bargain their way to the efficient quantity on

632
00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:00.560
their own, with no government at all.

633
00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:02.220
And here's the striking part.

634
00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:04.720
It doesn't matter who initially holds the right.

635
00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:06.960
Who holds the right determines who pays whom.

636
00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.480
But it does not change the efficient quantity.

637
00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:12.840
Coase fails, though, when there are too many parties involved,

638
00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.800
when rights are unclear or when bargaining is expensive.

639
00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:18.900
That's exactly why it doesn't solve climate change.

640
00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:21.980
The other private solution is simply doing the right thing.

641
00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:25.380
Social norms and conscience internalize some externalities

642
00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:26.880
without any policy at all.

643
00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:28.080
Government solutions.

644
00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:32.120
Command and control policies are direct rules, caps, bans,

645
00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:33.540
mandated technologies.

646
00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:37.460
Market-based policies are taxes, subsidies, and tradable

647
00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:37.880
permits.

648
00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:41.040
They change prices so that private incentives line up with

649
00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:41.760
social ones,

650
00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.200
and then let firms figure out how to respond.

651
00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.940
For this course you don't need the full tax and subsidy

652
00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:47.280
modeling.

653
00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:50.800
You just need to recognize why those count as market-based.

654
00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:52.860
Section 9. Chapter 12.

655
00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:53.600
Monopoly.

656
00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:56.260
Note that chapters 10 and 11 are skipped.

657
00:25:56.440 --> 00:25:59.940
Market power is the ability to set price above marginal cost.

658
00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:01.840
And it's a spectrum, not a switch.

659
00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:05.360
Perfect competition sits at one end, with zero market power,

660
00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:07.100
where firms are price-takers.

661
00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:09.180
Pure monopoly sits at the other end.

662
00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:11.480
Two things push a firm along that spectrum.

663
00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:14.960
How unique its good is, and how strong the barriers to entry

664
00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:15.240
are.

665
00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:19.320
Legal barriers include patents, licenses, and government-granted

666
00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:20.120
franchises.

667
00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:24.420
Natural barriers include huge economies of scale, control of a

668
00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.540
key resource, and network effects.

669
00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.200
Now, the most important idea in this chapter.

670
00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:32.640
Why does the monopolist's marginal revenue curve lie below its

671
00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:33.400
demand curve?

672
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:36.800
Because a monopolist is the entire market, to sell one more

673
00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.540
unit, it has to lower the price,

674
00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:42.080
and not just on that new unit, but on every unit it sells.

675
00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:45.320
So marginal revenue is the price of the new unit minus the

676
00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:47.240
revenue lost on all the previous ones.

677
00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:50.880
For a straight-line demand curve, marginal revenue has the same

678
00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:51.800
vertical intercept,

679
00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:55.760
but twice the slope, so it hits the horizontal axis at half the

680
00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:56.160
quantity.

681
00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:58.240
Now picture the monopoly graph.

682
00:26:58.500 --> 00:26:59.620
Demand slopes down.

683
00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:03.220
Marginal revenue slopes down twice as steeply, sitting below

684
00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:03.640
demand.

685
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:07.440
Marginal cost, for simplicity, is a flat horizontal line.

686
00:27:07.620 --> 00:27:09.380
Solving it is a two-step move.

687
00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:10.200
Step 1.

688
00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:13.960
Find the quantity where marginal revenue equals marginal cost.

689
00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:14.760
Step 2.

690
00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:17.560
Go straight up from that quantity to the demand curve,

691
00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.560
and read the price off the demand curve.

692
00:27:19.700 --> 00:27:22.540
Compare to a competitive market, where price would equal

693
00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:23.440
marginal cost.

694
00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:26.300
The monopolist produces less and charges more.

695
00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:27.940
Consumer surplus shrinks.

696
00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:31.360
Part of what consumers lost transfers to the firm as producer

697
00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:31.880
surplus.

698
00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:34.120
But the rest of it becomes deadweight loss.

699
00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:37.340
Mutually beneficial trades that simply never happen.

700
00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:40.960
The classic mistake, and I want you to say this out loud once,

701
00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:44.820
never set price, where marginal revenue equals marginal cost.

702
00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:47.320
That intersection gives you the quantity only.

703
00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.600
Price always comes off the demand curve,

704
00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:52.880
because that's what buyers are actually willing to pay for that

705
00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:53.280
quantity.

706
00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:55.680
How do you fix monopoly's deadweight loss?

707
00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:56.520
Two ways.

708
00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:58.560
The private fix is price discrimination.

709
00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:02.360
You only need to know perfect price discrimination, also called

710
00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:02.940
first degree,

711
00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:06.020
where the firm charges every single buyer exactly their

712
00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:06.840
willingness to pay.

713
00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.040
And the result is genuinely surprising.

714
00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:13.220
The firm produces the efficient quantity, so deadweight loss is

715
00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:14.800
zero and the outcome is efficient.

716
00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:16.900
But consumer surplus is also zero.

717
00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:20.500
The firm captures every bit of the surplus as producer surplus.

718
00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:23.720
So it's perfectly efficient and completely inequitable.

719
00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:26.740
That's the cleanest example in the course that efficient does

720
00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:27.520
not mean fair,

721
00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:29.560
and it's a great multiple-choice question.

722
00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:33.600
The policy fixes are trust-busting, meaning breaking firms up or

723
00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:34.460
blocking mergers,

724
00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:38.140
and price controls, meaning regulating the price down toward

725
00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:39.100
marginal cost.

726
00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:40.000
Section 10.

727
00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:41.160
Chapter 13.

728
00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:42.000
Game Theory.

729
00:28:42.200 --> 00:28:44.380
Game theory covers strategic situations,

730
00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:47.580
where your best move depends on what someone else does.

731
00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:51.240
Every game has three ingredients, players, strategies, and

732
00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:51.660
payoffs.

733
00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:53.400
You cover two types of games.

734
00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:54.180
Type 1.

735
00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:57.600
Simultaneous move games, shown with a payoff matrix.

736
00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:00.900
Both players move at the same time, or at least without seeing

737
00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:01.840
the other's choice.

738
00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:05.720
A best response is your best strategy given a specific choice by

739
00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:06.420
the other player.

740
00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:09.900
A Nash Equilibrium is a pair of strategies where both players

741
00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.220
are playing a best response,

742
00:29:11.420 --> 00:29:13.720
so neither one would want to unilaterally switch.

743
00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:16.960
Notice that this is just the course's general definition of

744
00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:18.680
equilibrium, applied to games.

745
00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:22.180
A dominant strategy is a strategy that's your best response no

746
00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:23.880
matter what the other player does.

747
00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:27.100
And you don't need the phrase dominant strategy equilibrium.

748
00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:30.960
He says that's just a Nash Equilibrium with a fancier name.

749
00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:32.880
Here's the procedure for solving one.

750
00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:36.460
Take player 1's payoffs, and for each of player 2's columns,

751
00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:38.420
mark player 1's best choice.

752
00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:42.000
Then take player 2's payoffs, and for each of player 1's rows,

753
00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.000
mark player 2's best choice.

754
00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:47.180
Any cell where both are marked is a Nash Equilibrium.

755
00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:48.860
Let's do the Prisoner's Dilemma.

756
00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:52.540
Two players, each choosing to stay quiet or to think, meaning in

757
00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:53.240
form on the other.

758
00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:55.660
If both stay quiet, each gets two.

759
00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:57.800
If both think, each gets one.

760
00:29:57.800 --> 00:30:01.380
If one thinks while the other stays quiet, the thinker gets

761
00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:03.020
three and the quiet one gets zero.

762
00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:04.100
Now check it.

763
00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:07.740
If player 2 stays quiet, player 1 gets three from thinking

764
00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:09.320
versus two from staying quiet.

765
00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:10.860
So player 1 thinks.

766
00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:14.900
If player 2 thinks, player 1 gets one from thinking versus zero

767
00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:15.760
from staying quiet.

768
00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:17.600
So player 1 thinks again.

769
00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:19.720
Thinking is a best response either way.

770
00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:23.340
So it's a dominant strategy for player 1, and by symmetry for

771
00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:24.340
player 2 as well.

772
00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.500
So both think, and each gets one.

773
00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.940
That's the unique Nash Equilibrium, even though both players

774
00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.800
would have been better off at 2 apiece by staying quiet.

775
00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:36.300
That gap between individually rational choices and the group

776
00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:38.440
outcome is the entire point of the dilemma.

777
00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:39.880
Two more things to know.

778
00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:43.920
Some games have exactly one Nash Equilibrium, some have several,

779
00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:46.280
and some have none in the sense you've defined.

780
00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:49.800
When there is none, players mix randomly between strategies,

781
00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:51.740
like in the penalty kick example.

782
00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:53.980
That's all you need on mixed strategies.

783
00:30:54.440 --> 00:30:57.960
Also a Nash Equilibrium can be understood as a social norm.

784
00:30:58.420 --> 00:31:01.560
A self-enforcing convention that nobody wants to break on their

785
00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:01.840
own.

786
00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:02.580
Type 2.

787
00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:05.340
Extensive form games, shown with a game tree.

788
00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:09.120
Here players move in sequence, so the second mover can see what

789
00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:09.980
the first one did.

790
00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:11.940
You solve these with backward induction.

791
00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:13.700
Start at the very end of the tree.

792
00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:17.080
Figure out what the last player would do at each decision node.

793
00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:19.380
Cross off the branches they would never take.

794
00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.920
Then work backwards, knowing exactly how they'll respond.

795
00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:24.060
Here's an example.

796
00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:26.320
Firma decides whether to enter a market.

797
00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:30.240
If a stays out, a gets 0 and the incumbent firm b gets 6.

798
00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:33.500
If a enters, then b chooses whether to fight or accommodate.

799
00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:37.340
If b fights, a gets negative 2 and b gets negative 1.

800
00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:40.440
If b accommodates, a gets 3 and b gets 2.

801
00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:41.580
Work backwards.

802
00:31:41.780 --> 00:31:45.760
If a enters, b compares fighting, which gives negative 1, to

803
00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:47.400
accommodating, which gives 2.

804
00:31:47.600 --> 00:31:48.500
b accommodates.

805
00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:53.080
So a's real choice is, enter and get 3, or stay out and get 0.

806
00:31:53.240 --> 00:31:53.700
Enters.

807
00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:55.820
The solution is enter, then accommodate.

808
00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:58.840
And the lesson is that b's threat to fight was never credible,

809
00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:01.220
because carrying it out would have hurt b.

810
00:32:01.420 --> 00:32:04.560
You'll also see two games borrowed from later chapters as

811
00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:05.020
examples.

812
00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:07.740
The fairness game and the trust and revenge game.

813
00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:11.380
Both show real people making choices that pure self-interest

814
00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:12.220
doesn't predict.

815
00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:13.020
Section 11.

816
00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:14.160
Closing topics.

817
00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:15.600
Competition and information.

818
00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:19.860
These come from textbook chapters 14 and 16, but only at the

819
00:32:19.860 --> 00:32:20.580
slide level.

820
00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:23.140
You can ignore most of the detail in those chapters.

821
00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:24.540
First, competition.

822
00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:27.680
Results along the market power spectrum interpolate.

823
00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.860
That means a market sitting between perfect competition and

824
00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:32.840
monopoly gets an outcome between the two.

825
00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:34.980
Some mark up over marginal cost.

826
00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:36.460
Some dead weight loss.

827
00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:39.240
But less of each than a pure monopoly would produce.

828
00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:42.680
Collusion is when rival firms cooperate to act like a single

829
00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:44.440
monopolist and keep prices high.

830
00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.340
Notice that collusion is itself a prisoner's dilemma.

831
00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.900
Each firm's private incentive is to cheat and quietly undercut

832
00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:51.460
the others.

833
00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:54.160
That's exactly why cartels are unstable.

834
00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:57.880
Next, you don't actually need many firms to get a healthy level

835
00:32:57.880 --> 00:32:58.540
of competition.

836
00:32:58.820 --> 00:33:01.380
A handful of genuine rivals goes a long way.

837
00:33:01.540 --> 00:33:04.840
And critically, the number of firms is not very informative

838
00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:06.420
about market concentration.

839
00:33:06.780 --> 00:33:07.460
Market shares.

840
00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:11.640
20 firms where one of them holds 95% is not a competitive

841
00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:12.060
market.

842
00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:14.000
Which brings us to the HHI.

843
00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:15.900
The Herfindahl-Hirschman Index.

844
00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:19.860
You calculate it by squaring each firm's market share, expressed

845
00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:21.600
as a percentage, and summing them.

846
00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:26.960
So four firms with 25% each gives you 4 times 625, which equals

847
00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:27.940
2,500.

848
00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:31.540
A single firm with 100% gives you 10,000.

849
00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:33.320
Higher means more concentrated.

850
00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:36.760
It captures share rather than a simple firm count, which is

851
00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:38.560
exactly why regulators use it.

852
00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:40.660
Now, asymmetric information.

853
00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:44.260
Asymmetric information means one side of a transaction knows

854
00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:45.720
something the other side doesn't.

855
00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:49.620
It creates two distinct problems, and the exam will absolutely

856
00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:51.020
want you to tell them apart.

857
00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:53.460
Adverse selection is a hidden type problem.

858
00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:56.460
It happens before the deal, and it's about who signs up.

859
00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:59.740
The classic example is that only sick people buy the insurance

860
00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:01.920
because they're the ones who know they'll need it.

861
00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:04.080
Moral hazard is a hidden action problem.

862
00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:07.540
It happens after the deal, and it's about how people behave once

863
00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:08.140
they're covered.

864
00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:11.080
The classic example is that insured drivers drive more

865
00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:11.580
recklessly.

866
00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:15.040
So, hidden type before the deal versus hidden action after the

867
00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:15.300
deal.

868
00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:16.400
That's the distinction.

869
00:34:16.700 --> 00:34:20.140
The death spiral is what adverse selection does when left alone.

870
00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:23.000
Insurers raise premiums to cover a sick pool.

871
00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:26.320
The healthiest remaining people decide it's no longer worth it

872
00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:27.000
and drop out.

873
00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:28.120
The pool gets sicker.

874
00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:29.400
Premiums rise again.

875
00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:29.880
Repeat.

876
00:34:30.140 --> 00:34:32.180
Until the market can collapse entirely.

877
00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:34.840
Fixing either problem starts the same way.

878
00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:36.700
Fix the information asymmetry.

879
00:34:36.940 --> 00:34:39.700
For moral hazards specifically, there's a second option.

880
00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:42.880
If the principal can design a contract or agreement so that the

881
00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:46.260
agent's incentives more closely match the principals, that also

882
00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:47.320
solves the problem.

883
00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:50.000
Think commission pay or an insurance deductible.

884
00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:53.340
That brings up the principal-agent relationship, where one

885
00:34:53.340 --> 00:34:56.440
party, the agent, acts on behalf of another, the principal,

886
00:34:56.700 --> 00:35:00.340
employee and employer, contractor and homeowner, doctor and

887
00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:00.700
patient.

888
00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:03.620
But be careful here, because this is a deliberate trap.

889
00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:06.680
Not every principal-agent relationship suffers from moral

890
00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:07.100
hazard.

891
00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:10.340
It only becomes moral hazard when the principal cannot observe

892
00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:12.240
what the agent is actually doing.

893
00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:15.040
If you can see the work, there's no hidden action.

894
00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:18.260
That distinction is exactly the kind of thing he writes multiple

895
00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:19.520
-choice questions around.

896
00:35:19.760 --> 00:35:20.360
Final section.

897
00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:23.580
The formula review and the traps that cost the most points.

898
00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:24.800
The formula is quickly.

899
00:35:25.060 --> 00:35:26.020
Midpoint elasticity.

900
00:35:35.860 --> 00:35:38.660
Total cost.

901
00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:57.500
Total cost.

902
00:35:58.060 --> 00:36:01.000
Or equivalently, quantity times the gap between price and

903
00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:02.100
average total cost.

904
00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:06.220
Under perfect competition, price equals marginal revenue equals

905
00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:07.040
average revenue.

906
00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:09.740
Produce where price equals marginal cost.

907
00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:12.900
The long-run price equals minimum average total cost.

908
00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:16.060
Shut down in the short run if price is below minimum average

909
00:36:16.060 --> 00:36:17.000
variable cost.

910
00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:20.380
Exit in the long run if price is below minimum average total

911
00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:20.820
cost.

912
00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:23.480
Break-even price is minimum average total cost.

913
00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:27.440
For any firm, competitive or not, produce where marginal revenue

914
00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:28.900
equals marginal cost.

915
00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:31.920
Consumer surplus is under demand and above price.

916
00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.380
Producer surplus is above marginal cost and below price.

917
00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:37.180
Total surplus is the sum.

918
00:36:37.340 --> 00:36:40.100
Triangle area is one-half times base times height.

919
00:36:40.300 --> 00:36:43.480
The social optimum is where marginal social benefit equals

920
00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:44.760
marginal social cost.

921
00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:48.040
The market outcome is where marginal private benefit equals

922
00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:49.300
marginal private cost.

923
00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:52.920
And HHI is the sum of squared percentage market shares.

924
00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:55.920
Now, the ten traps that cost the most points.

925
00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:56.420
1.

926
00:36:56.680 --> 00:36:57.700
Shift versus movement.

927
00:36:57.940 --> 00:37:00.800
If the good zone price changed, move along the curve.

928
00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:01.720
Anything else.

929
00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:02.840
Shift the curve.

930
00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:03.200
2.

931
00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:04.180
Sunk costs.

932
00:37:04.400 --> 00:37:05.180
Already spent.

933
00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:05.960
Already gone.

934
00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:07.800
Never a reason to keep going.

935
00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:08.180
3.

936
00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:09.780
Marginals from tables.

937
00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:11.780
Divide by the change in quantity.

938
00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:13.560
Don't just subtract rows.

939
00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:14.100
4.

940
00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:15.220
Monopoly price.

941
00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:18.560
Get the quantity from marginal revenue equals marginal cost.

942
00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:21.200
Then get the price from the demand curve above it.

943
00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:21.680
5.

944
00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:23.520
Elasticity is not slope.

945
00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:26.740
A straight demand curve has one slope and many different

946
00:37:26.740 --> 00:37:27.520
elasticities.

947
00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:28.000
6.

948
00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:30.180
Economic versus accounting profit.

949
00:37:30.400 --> 00:37:30.760
0.

950
00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:34.100
Economic profit is normal and completely fine in long-run

951
00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:34.640
competition.

952
00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:37.720
It means you're earning exactly what you could earn elsewhere.

953
00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:38.460
7.

954
00:37:38.780 --> 00:37:40.220
Efficient does not mean fair.

955
00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:43.440
Perfect price discrimination is the cleanest counterexample.

956
00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.020
0.

957
00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:44.900
Deadweight loss.

958
00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:45.480
0.

959
00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:46.520
Consumer surplus.

960
00:37:47.120 --> 00:37:47.260
8.

961
00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:49.080
Positive versus normative.

962
00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:51.440
Positive means falsifiable in principle.

963
00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:53.240
It does not mean true.

964
00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:53.940
9.

965
00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:55.260
Ceiling versus floor.

966
00:37:55.480 --> 00:37:58.820
A ceiling sits below equilibrium and creates excess demand.

967
00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:02.200
A floor sits above equilibrium and creates excess supply.

968
00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:04.660
Draw the horizontal line before you answer.

969
00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:05.220
10.

970
00:38:05.660 --> 00:38:07.660
Adverse election versus moral hazard.

971
00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:09.420
Hidden tight before the deal.

972
00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:10.620
Hidden action after it.

973
00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:11.840
That's the whole course.

974
00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:14.540
Use this to get the building blocks down fast.

975
00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:16.600
On your walk to class or at the gym.

976
00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:19.620
Then go do the part that actually moves a B to an A.

977
00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:23.680
Work the practice exams cold, with no notes, and rewrite every

978
00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:26.280
question until you can see what each one is really testing.

979
00:38:26.540 --> 00:38:26.960
Good luck.
